We used the self-hosted runner because of how we designed your CI/CD lab.

In our setup
                 GitHub
                   │
             GitHub Actions
                   │
                   ▼
        ┌─────────────────────┐
        │ Self-hosted Runner  │
        │      k8-m1           │
        └──────────┬──────────┘
                   │
          Docker build/push
                   │
                   ▼
              Docker Hub
                   │
                   │ kubectl apply
                   ▼
        ┌─────────────────────┐
        │ Kubernetes Cluster  │
        │                     │
        │ k8-m1  +  k8-worker │
        └─────────────────────┘
Why did we choose self-hosted?

Your Kubernetes cluster is running on your own Azure VMs, and we installed the GitHub runner on k8-m1.

The runner gives GitHub Actions a machine that can directly execute:

docker build
docker push
kubectl apply
kubectl get pods

So the runner is basically the worker machine for GitHub Actions.

Could we use GitHub-hosted runner instead?

Yes, absolutely.

You could use:

runs-on: ubuntu-latest

instead of:

runs-on: self-hosted

Then GitHub gives you a temporary Ubuntu VM.

But there is an important difference:

GitHub-hosted runner
       │
       │ Internet
       ▼
   Your K8 cluster

Your Kubernetes API needs to be reachable from that runner.

With our self-hosted runner:

GitHub
   │
   │ job
   ▼
k8-m1 runner
   │
   │ private network
   ▼
Kubernetes API

That's why self-hosted is useful for private Kubernetes clusters.

In your lab

We put the runner on k8-m1 mainly to learn the real-world pattern:

GitHub → CI/CD runner → private Kubernetes cluster

The runner itself is not part of Kubernetes.

Think of it this way:

Component	Purpose
Kubernetes	Runs your application
NGINX Ingress	Receives application traffic
Azure Load Balancer	Exposes the cluster
Docker Hub	Stores container images
GitHub Actions	Automates CI/CD
Self-hosted runner	Executes the GitHub Actions commands

So if an interviewer asks:

"Why did you use a self-hosted GitHub Actions runner?"

A good answer is:

"The Kubernetes cluster was running in a private network, so I used a self-hosted GitHub Actions runner inside the environment. This allowed the pipeline to execute Docker and kubectl commands against the private Kubernetes cluster without exposing the Kubernetes API publicly."

That is the main reason.
